    "knowledge_point_id": "3c547746-49c8-5a4a-85fe-c6b75493a354",
    "knowledge_point": "Differentiating a Quotient Containing Other Functions",
    "problem": "If a curve $C$ is given by $y=\\frac{sin⁡x}{2x},$ calculate the slope of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $x=\\pi .$",
    "worked_solution": "In this case, the expression for $y$ is a quotient $\\frac{u(x)}{v(x)}$ with $u(x)=sin⁡x$ and $v(x)=2x.$ \n\nTherefore, we apply the quotient rule as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{{u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(sin⁡x)^{′}\\cdot (2x)-sin⁡x\\cdot (2x)^{′}}{(2x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{cos⁡x\\cdot (2x)-sin⁡x\\cdot 2}{{(2x)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(xcos⁡x-sin⁡x)}{4{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{xcos⁡x-sin⁡x}{2{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we now substitute $x=\\pi $ into the formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(\\pi ) & =\\frac{\\pi cos⁡(\\pi )-sin⁡(\\pi )}{2(\\pi )^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi (-1)-(0)}{2{\\pi }^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{2{\\pi }^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line is $-\\frac{1}{2\\pi }.$",
